To write a function in Rust that reverses a string, you can use the following code snippet as a guide:

```rust
fn reverse_string(input: &str) -> String {
    // Convert the input string into a collection of characters
    let mut chars: Vec<char> = input.chars().collect();

    // Reverse the order of characters
    chars.reverse();

    // Convert the reversed characters back into a string
    let reversed_string: String = chars.iter().collect();

    // Return the reversed string
    return reversed_string;
}

fn main() {
    let original_string = "hello";
    let reversed_string = reverse_string(original_string);
    println!("{}", reversed_string);
}
```

In this Rust function, we first convert the input string into a vector of characters, reverse their order, and then collect them back into a new string. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this `reverse_string` function to reverse the string "hello" and print the result.

