To optimize the bubble sort algorithm so it doesn't iterate over elements that are already sorted, you can introduce a mechanism to reduce the range of the outer loop with each pass. Bubble sort has an inherent characteristic that the largest unsorted element "bubbles up" to its correct position with every pass through the list. Once an element is in its correct position, it doesn't need to be checked again in subsequent iterations. You can use a `lastSwapped` pointer or index to track the last position where a swap occurred during a pass, and in the next iteration, only sort up to that position. Here's how you can optimize your algorithm:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <iterator>
#include <algorithm>

template <typename RandomIter, typename Comparator = std::less<>>
void bubble(RandomIter begin, RandomIter end, Comparator comp = Comparator{}) {
    using difference_type = typename std::iterator_traits<RandomIter>::difference_type;

    std::cout << "start" << std::endl;
    std::cout << std::distance(begin, end) << std::endl;

    difference_type n = std::distance(begin, end);
    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }

    RandomIter lastSwapped;
    bool swapped = true;

    while (swapped) {
        swapped = false;
        lastSwapped = begin;

        for (RandomIter current = begin; current < end - 1; ++current) {
            RandomIter next = current + 1;
            if (comp(*next, *current)) {
                std::swap(*next, *current);
                swapped = true;
                lastSwapped = next;
            }
        }

        end = lastSwapped;  // Reduce the range of the next pass

        /*
        for (RandomIter k = begin; k < end; ++k) {
            std::cout << *k << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
        */
    }
}
```

### Key Changes:
1. **`swapped` Variable**: Check if any swaps were made during the pass. If no swaps are made, the list is sorted, and we can exit early.
2. **`lastSwapped` Iterator**: Track the last position where a swap occurred on each pass and reduce the end boundary for the next pass to that position. This is because after this position, it's guaranteed that the elements are in their correct positions.

This optimized version should be notably faster for inputs that are already or nearly sorted, as it will minimize unnecessary comparisons after the list is sorted.

