    half4 sum = half4(0.0);

    for (int i = -radius; i <= radius; i++)
    {
        for (int j = -radius; j <= radius; j++)
        {
            uint2 currentPixel = gid + uint2(i, j);

            if (currentPixel.x < inputImage.get_width() && currentPixel.y < inputImage.get_height())
            {
                half4 pixel = inputImage.read(currentPixel);
                int index = i + radius + (j + radius) * kernelSize;
                sum += pixel * kernel[index];
            }
        }
    }

    outputImage.write(sum, gid);
}
```

Explanation of code:  
1. The kernel function takes two input textures, one for reading the input image and one for writing the output image. It also takes a buffer containing the Gaussian blur sigma value.
2. It defines the Gaussian blur radius and kernel size.
3. It initializes the Gaussian kernel values.
4. It loops through the pixels in the radius of the current pixel and calculates the Gaussian blur value for each pixel.
5. It checks if the current pixel is within the bounds of the image before reading its value.
6. It writes the calculated Gaussian blur value to the output image.

