To create a Metal compute kernel for Gaussian blurring an image, we first need to define the kernel function that will perform the blurring operation. Let's assume a simple 5x5 Gaussian kernel for this example:

```metal
kernel void gaussianBlur(texture2d<half, access::sample> inputImage [[texture(0)]],
                         texture2d<half, access::write> outputImage [[texture(1)]],
                         uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]])
{
    constexpr sampler linearSampler(coord::pixel, address::clamp_to_edge);

    half4 resultColor = half4(0.0);

    for (int i = -2; i <= 2; i++)
    {
        for (int j = -2; j <= 2; j++)
        {
            uint2 currentPos = gid + uint2(i, j);

            half4 currentColor = inputImage.sample(linearSampler, currentPos);
            half weight = getGaussianWeight(i, j);

            resultColor += weight * currentColor;
        }
    }

    outputImage.write(resultColor, gid);
}

inline half getGaussianWeight(int x, int y)
{
    float sigma = 1.0; // Gaussian standard deviation
    return exp(-0.5 * (x * x + y * y) / (sigma * sigma));
}
```

In the kernel function above, we iterate through a 5x5 neighborhood around each pixel in the image, applying the Gaussian weights to calculate the resulting color. This pseudo-code should give you a general idea of the structure required for a Metal compute kernel for Gaussian blurring.

