To establish a one-to-many relationship between two entities in Java Spring using JPA, and ensure that the deletion of entity A deletes all related entity Bs, follow these steps:

1. **Entity A Setup**:
   - Annotate the collection representing entity Bs with `@OneToMany`.
   - Use the `cascade` attribute with `CascadeType.ALL` to propagate all operations, including delete.
   - Set `orphanRemoval = true` to automatically remove `entity Bs` without associations when `entity A` is deleted.

   ```java
   @Entity
   public class EntityA {
       @Id
       @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
       private Long id;

       @OneToMany(mappedBy = "entityA", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
       private List<EntityB> entityBs;

       // getters and setters
   }
   ```

2. **Entity B Setup**:
   - Annotate the reference to entity A with `@ManyToOne`.
   - Use `@JoinColumn` to define the foreign key column.

   ```java
   @Entity
   public class EntityB {
       @Id
       @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
       private Long id;

       @ManyToOne
       @JoinColumn(name = "entity_a_id")
       private EntityA entityA;

       // getters and setters
   }
   ```

This setup will automatically handle the deletion of `entity Bs` when an `entity A` is deleted using JPA repository methods.

