This setup gives a skeleton to start building a H.264 decoder. Each H.264 component, including predictions, transforms, and entropy decoding, will need a deep dive into the specification for accurate implementation. Consider referencing the H.264 standard (ITU-T H.264) and using existing open-source projects (like x264 or ffmpeg) as guides and for testing compliance.

\subsection{Human Evaluation}

In the domain of machine translation (MT), human evaluation plays a pivotal role in assessing the quality and performance of translation systems. This subsection delves into various dimensions of human evaluation, focusing on fluency, adequacy, Direct Assessment (DA), and the Metrics for Translation Quality Management (MQM). These metrics provide nuanced insights into the effectiveness of machine-translated content by leveraging human judgment, which remains the gold standard for quality assessment despite advancements in automated metrics.

\subsubsection{Fluency}

Fluency refers to the smoothness and naturalness of the translated text in the target language. It evaluates whether the translation adheres to grammatical rules and idiomatic expressions typical of a native speaker. Human evaluators assess fluency by examining factors such as syntax, morphology, and coherence. High fluency signifies that the translation reads naturally to a native speaker of the target language, without any awkward phrasing or grammatical errors. Evaluators typically rate fluency on a scale, providing quantifiable data that help in identifying the strengths and weaknesses of a machine translation system in crafting linguistically refined outputs.

\subsubsection{Adequacy}

Adequacy measures the degree to which the translated text conveys the intended meaning of the source text. This dimension focuses on the faithfulness of the translation, ensuring that all semantic elements and nuances are preserved. Human evaluators assess adequacy by comparing the source and target texts, often dealing with the challenges of semantic equivalence and cultural nuances. A high adequacy score indicates that the translation successfully retains the original message, with minimal loss or distortion of content. Adequacy is instrumental in determining the reliability of a machine translation system in accurately representing source text information.

\subsubsection{Direct Assessment (DA)}

Direct Assessment (DA) is a method of human evaluation that provides a holistic score based on the overall quality of the translation. It requires evaluators to consider both fluency and adequacy simultaneously, allowing for a more integrated appraisal of translation quality. DA typically involves rating the translation on a numeric scale, offering a direct and aggregated measure of translation performance. This approach has gained traction for its simplicity and efficiency, providing a quick yet comprehensive evaluation of machine-translated outputs. DA is particularly useful in large-scale studies where a streamlined assessment method can significantly enhance the evaluation process.

\subsubsection{Metrics for Translation Quality Management (MQM)}

Metrics for Translation Quality Management (MQM) is a framework that combines a set of standardized criteria for evaluating translation quality. MQM offers a comprehensive approach by encompassing various translation errors, categorizing them into linguistic, transfer, and content adequacy issues, among others. Human evaluators utilize MQM by annotating translated outputs with specific error types, facilitating a detailed diagnostic of the translation system's performance. This methodology not only helps identify areas for improvement but also guides the development of more refined translation models. MQM's structured approach is invaluable for research and commercial applications, providing a systematic means to manage and enhance translation quality across diverse languages and domains.

