If you need to throw checked exceptions from inside a lambda expression, you have a few options:

1. **Handle the Exception Within the Lambda:**
   Catch the checked exception inside the lambda and handle it within the lambda body.

   ```java
   List<String> list = Arrays.asList("1", "2", "a");

   list.forEach(item -> {
       try {
           int number = Integer.parseInt(item);
           System.out.println(number);
       } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
           System.out.println("Invalid number format: " + item);
       }
   });
   ```

2. **Use a Wrapper Functional Interface:**
   Create your own functional interface that allows throwing checked exceptions, and a utility method to wrap it.

   ```java
   @FunctionalInterface
   interface CheckedConsumer<T> {
       void accept(T t) throws Exception;
   }

   static <T> Consumer<T> wrapCheckedConsumer(CheckedConsumer<T> checkedConsumer) {
       return i -> {
           try {
               checkedConsumer.accept(i);
           } catch (Exception e) {
               throw new RuntimeException(e);
           }
       };
