    gap: 0.2em;
}

.word {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 18px; /* Adjust as needed */
}

.word::after, .word::before, .word .underline-group span {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    width: 100%;
    height: 3px; /* Thickness of the underline */
    left: 0;
    background-color: transparent;
}

.word[data-categories~="cat1"]::after {
    top: 100%;
    background-color: blue; /* Color for category 1 */
    z-index: 1;
}

.word[data-categories~="cat2"]::before {
    top: 105%; /* Adjust vertical position */
    background-color: red; /* Color for category 2 */
    z-index: 2;
}

.word[data-categories~="cat3"] .underline-group span {
    top: 110%; /* Adjust vertical position */
    background-color: green; /* Color for category 3 */
    z-index: 3;
}
```

### JavaScript (script.js)
You can use JavaScript to dynamically add classes or modify data attributes based on the categories of each word. This example doesn't require any JavaScript unless you want to add interactivity, like toggling categories. Here's a basic setup.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const words = document.querySelectorAll('.word');

    words.forEach(word => {
        const categories = word.getAttribute('data-categories').split(' ');

        // Create a span for additional underline layers if needed
        if (categories.includes('cat3')) {
            const underlineGroup = document.createElement('div');
            underlineGroup.classList.add('underline-group');
            const underlineSpan = document.createElement('span');
            underlineGroup.appendChild(underlineSpan);
            word.appendChild(underlineGroup);
        }
    });
});
```

### Explanation
- **HTML**: Each word is wrapped in a `<span>` with a `data-categories` attribute to specify its categories.
- **CSS**: The underlines are created using CSS pseudo-elements and positioned absolutely under each word. Each category has its own color and vertical offset.
- **JavaScript**: If dynamic behavior is needed, JavaScript can manipulate the DOM to add or adjust the styles or categories dynamically.

This setup may require adjustments depending on the specific requirements or if more categories are added. Adjust the `top` values and colors in the CSS to prevent overlaps and maintain readability.

