Creating a React component that includes a form for users to fill out personal information and sends the form data to an Express API involves several steps. Here's a complete example that demonstrates how to create such a form and handle form submissions using modern JavaScript practices, including `fetch` and `async/await`.

```jsx
// PersonalInfoForm.js
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const PersonalInfoForm = () => {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({
    name: '',
    birthday: '',
    email: '',
    phoneNumber: '',
  });

  const [message, setMessage] = useState('');

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const { name, value } = event.target;
    setFormData((prevData) => ({
      ...prevData,
      [name]: value,
    }));
  };

  const handleSubmit = async (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();

    try {
      const response = await fetch('http://localhost:5000/api/submit', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
        body: JSON.stringify(formData),
      });

      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
      }

      const responseData = await response.json();
      setMessage('Form successfully submitted!');
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error submitting form:', error);
      setMessage('There was a problem submitting the form. Please try again later.');
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Personal Information Form</h2>
      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
        <div>
          <label htmlFor="name">Name:</label>
          <input
            type="text"
            id="name"
            name="name"
            value={formData.name}
            onChange={handleChange}
            required
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <label htmlFor="birthday">Birthday:</label>
          <input
            type="date"
            id="birthday"
            name="birthday"
            value={formData.birthday}
            onChange={handleChange}
            required
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <label htmlFor="email">Email:</label>
          <input
            type="email"
            id="email"
            name="email"
            value={formData.email}
            onChange={handleChange}
            required
          />
        </div>
        <div>
          <label htmlFor="phoneNumber">Phone Number:</label>
          <input
            type="tel"
            id="phoneNumber"
            name="phoneNumber"
            value={formData.phoneNumber}
            onChange={handleChange}
            required
          />
        </div>
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
      </form>
      {message && <p>{message}</p>}
    </div>
  );
};

export default PersonalInfoForm;
```

### Explanation:

- **State Management:** The form data is managed using the `useState` hook. This hook allows you to create a state variable `formData` to store the input values.

- **Form Structure:** The form contains inputs for `name`, `birthday`, `email`, and `phoneNumber`. Each input has a `name` attribute that matches its corresponding property in the `formData` state object.

- **Event Handling:**
  - `handleChange` updates the state with the current value of the input whenever a change is made.
  - `handleSubmit` is an asynchronous function that prevents the default form submission, sends a POST request using `fetch`, and handles any errors that might occur during this process.

- **Error Handling:** Errors during the fetch request are caught and handled, providing user feedback.

Make sure your Express API is running on `http://localhost:5000` and has a `/api/submit` endpoint set up to handle POST requests and process the incoming data.

