In Swing (Java's GUI toolkit), `JTextArea` does not directly support text alignment or centering, as it is typically used for multi-line, plain-text editing with a left alignment by default. However, you can achieve centered text in a `JTextArea` by using a workaround. One common approach is to use a `StyledDocument` with a `JTextPane` instead, which does support more complex styling options, including text alignment. Here's how you can center text using `JTextPane`:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import javax.swing.text.*;

public class CenterTextInJTextPane {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a JFrame
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Center Text Example");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.setSize(400, 300);

        // Create a JTextPane
        JTextPane textPane = new JTextPane();

        // Create a StyledDocument to hold the properties of the text
        StyledDocument doc = textPane.getStyledDocument();

        // Create a style for the text
        SimpleAttributeSet center = new SimpleAttributeSet();

        // Set the alignment to center
        StyleConstants.setAlignment(center, StyleConstants.ALIGN_CENTER);

        // Apply the alignment to the document's logical view
        doc.setParagraphAttributes(0, doc.getLength(), center, false);

        // Set the text
        textPane.setText("This is centered text in a JTextPane.");

        // Add the JTextPane to a JScrollPane for better usability
        JScrollPane scrollPane = new JScrollPane(textPane);

        // Add the scrollPane to the frame
        frame.add(scrollPane);

        // Make the frame visible
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

In this example, we're creating a `JTextPane` instead of a `JTextArea`, and we're using a `StyledDocument` to manage the text's properties. We apply a `SimpleAttributeSet` to set the text alignment to center using `StyleConstants.setAlignment`.

If you specifically need to use `JTextArea` and want similar functionality, you would have to implement a custom painting mechanism, but for most purposes, switching to `JTextPane` as shown above is a more straightforward and practical approach.

